<h1>Video 070 — Exception handling introduction</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Exception Handling Part-1 || Introduction</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/070-zg4jYJjNEYI.txt). ASR decoded in place: exception ending / exceptional handling = exception handling; entry room / entryum = interview; comp / comps / compile errors = compile-time errors; finally PL = finally block; T with resources = try-with-resources; keep supporting / keep subing = keep copying (board); Wanastali purum = Vanasthalipuram; atagar / asanagar / Saranagar = Ameerpet; Mipiatam / Maidipam / Adipatam / Modiam / miatam = Madhapur; massup tank = Masab Tank; two-heer bike = two-wheeler; tire got punched / punchured / punchard = tire got punctured; power gun = power gone; slipping exception = SleepingException (classroom name, not in the API); locating get London = located at London; file not found exception = FileNotFoundException; SQL exception = SQLException; public static v / y main string array accept = public static void main(String[] args); do stuff / do stop = doStuff(); do more stuff / do more of = doMoreStuff(); SOP / system.out.print print len = System.out.println; runtime ST / step = runtime stack; cmd = created (runtime stack for main thread); class test analy = class Test only; huh m = matinee (morning-show vs evening-show ticket joke).</p>
<p>This video is only introduction + runtime stack mechanism on a happy-path program. He announces default exception handling as the next heading at 54:26 and stops. Do not invent JVM default-handler / printStackTrace / main dying details here.</p>
<h3>00:04 — Next topic: exception handling (his favorite)</h3>
<p>Next topic: exception handling. He calls it the most valuable concept in the total Java. It is one of his favorite topics. After this series you should get each and every point related to exception handling.</p>
<p>He starts with the agenda — he says the agenda itself is strong; you can cross-check against it later.</p>
<h3>00:37 — Spoken agenda (the full series, not this video)</h3>
<p>He lists what the whole exception-handling series will cover. This Part-1 video does not teach items after introduction + runtime stack. He is only spelling the map:</p>
<ol><li>What is exception and what is the meaning of exception handling.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Runtime stack mechanism — for every thread a runtime stack is created; how that stack plays a role in exception handling.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Default exception handling in Java — if you are not handling any exception, what is Java doing by default.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Exception hierarchy — most valuable: Throwable is the parent; Exception and Error are children. Difference between exception and error. Checked exception vs unchecked exception.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Customized exception handling by using try-catch — role of try and catch.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Control flow in try-catch — within try, if an exception rises and catch matches, what happens; if catch does not match, what happens; if there is no exception, what is the flow.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Methods to print exception information — three methods.</li></ol>
<ol><li>try with multiple catch blocks — you can take multiple catch blocks for the same try; in which situation, and which rules.</li></ol>
<ol><li>finally block.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Interview favorite: difference between `final`, `finally`, `finalize`.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Control flow in try-catch-finally (he already listed control flow in try-catch; this is the finally version).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Control flow in nested try-catch-finally — within try another try-catch-finally.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Various possible combinations of try-catch-finally — almost 24 or 25 combinations: try-catch-finally, try without catch, finally without try, try-catch inside finally, and so on.</li></ol>
<ol><li>`throw` and `throws` — throw keyword, throws keyword.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Exception-handling keyword summary — what are the various exception-handling keywords.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Various possible compile errors in exception handling — almost eight or nine compile errors; every compile error itself is an important interview question.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Customized / user-defined exceptions (our own exceptions) — how to define, how to use.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Ten exception case studies — ten commonly occurring exceptions. Example he names: NullPointerException — when do we get it, explain with an example, checked or unchecked, behavior.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Java 1.7 enhancements — two things: try-with-resources, and multi-catch block.</li></ol>
<p>He slips “this is about agenda for multi-threading” then immediately talks 20 / 21 / 22 exception subtopics. Decode: this agenda is for exception handling, not multi-threading.</p>
<p>Guarantee he gives: after these ~20 topics you can declare yourself king in exception handling.</p>
<h3>04:56 — Board time: copy the agenda (long pause)</h3>
<p>Classroom copy. He says “take clearly.” Applause / silence while the board is written. Same list as above, now as a board heading list:</p>
<ul><li>Introduction</li></ul>
<ul><li>Runtime stack mechanism</li></ul>
<ul><li>Default exception handling in Java</li></ul>
<ul><li>Exception hierarchy</li></ul>
<ul><li>Customized exception handling by using try-catch</li></ul>
<ul><li>Control flow in try-catch</li></ul>
<ul><li>Methods to print exception information</li></ul>
<ul><li>try with multiple catch blocks</li></ul>
<ul><li>finally block</li></ul>
<ul><li>(final vs finally vs finalize was spoken earlier; he is still on the same agenda)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Control flow in try-catch-finally / nested try-catch-finally / combinations</li></ul>
<ul><li>throw / throws / keyword summary / compile errors / user-defined exceptions / 10 case studies</li></ul>
<ul><li>Java 1.7: try-with-resources, multi-catch</li></ul>
<h3>08:32 — Agenda is itself a big topic</h3>
<p>He says the agenda itself looks like a big topic. Count on the board: 17 subtopics, then he says almost 20 subtopics. Time estimate: around 15 to 20 hours on exception handling.</p>
<p>He re-reads the first block slowly so they copy:</p>
<ul><li>Introduction</li></ul>
<ul><li>Runtime stack mechanism</li></ul>
<ul><li>Default exception handling in Java</li></ul>
<ul><li>Exception hierarchy</li></ul>
